Patch to upstream OpenCV: fix v_round(v_float32x4) in the wasm SIMD backend.

Upstream (the 4.x and 5.x branches, unfixed as of 5.0.0) computes trunc(a + 0.5): every
negative value comes out 1 too high (-20.0 → -19), .5 rounds up (114.5 → 115), and even
0.49999997f first rounds to 1.0f in single precision when 0.5 is added, then truncates
to 1. The scalar path's cvRound() rounds half to even. So the SIMD variant disagreed with
baseline, and with OpenCV's own semantics, in every kernel that rounds floating point to
integers: addWeighted, convertTo with scaling, divide, multiply, GaussianBlur / blur /
resize / Sobel on 16S, and the addConstant / mulConstant / constantSubtract /
constantDivide built on them.

The fix rounds with f32x4.nearest (IEEE roundTiesToEven) before trunc_sat, which matches
cvRound bit for bit: checked in Node with a hand-assembled wasm module against -20 /
-115 / 114.5 / ±2.5 / ±3.5 / -0.5 / 0.49999997 / ±1e10 / NaN. The two "float → int
rounding" cases in test/variant-cases.js require baseline and simd to be bit-identical,
which an unpatched simd artifact fails.

Applied by build/build.sh after cloning the source; a patch that no longer applies fails
the build instead of silently producing an unpatched artifact.

--- a/modules/core/include/opencv2/core/hal/intrin_wasm.hpp
+++ b/modules/core/include/opencv2/core/hal/intrin_wasm.hpp
@@ -1930,8 +1930,9 @@
 
 inline v_int32x4 v_round(const v_float32x4& a)
 {
-    v128_t h = wasm_f32x4_splat(0.5);
-    return v_int32x4(wasm_i32x4_trunc_saturate_f32x4(wasm_f32x4_add(a.val, h)));
+    // Round half to even, like cvRound(). trunc(a + 0.5) was off by one for negative
+    // values (-20.0 -> -19) and broke ties away from even (114.5 -> 115).
+    return v_int32x4(wasm_i32x4_trunc_saturate_f32x4(wasm_f32x4_nearest(a.val)));
 }
 
 inline v_int32x4 v_floor(const v_float32x4& a)
